277 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117277 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117277 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5d9216ff-8580-5238-a204-fb582eb42893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117278 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b318a529-cb5c-5b37-96db-63c317afc535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117279 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117279 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117279 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f652fed8-2914-5671-88b8-ca2f9b7b3fdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117280 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "133d29b3-ba83-5073-9fb5-ae8a15891606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117281 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117281 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117281 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17eb303e-e00d-5d84-a4f8-d39cb49ec3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117282 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117282 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117282 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3695b530-f752-5009-8f87-09a944b9b94a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119603 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119603 #edb/ref 1071 "Given ![](images/e3/e34daf05ba492435f014fedd8336dd4bb4c65ae9c1688947d7873b8fba3b3e22.svg) find its LU decomposition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119603 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119604 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119604 #edb/ref 1071 "Given \n![](images/d5/d5f838110aeb104064744379d4879fb237a2ad9ad7b4728f4216d9f1bd39cd8a.svg), find its LU decomposition." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119604 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119668 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119668 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. ![](images/62/62e90ac708f5a7d2942d328a1dcbcfc435e6cf900b71f0564307e020d822748b.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/94/94db4ea75dacbd6aaf0ab0e32a6f871bc6e50c8029da7c6800a49762feb2f560.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/e4/e4b8dd6cb97a21581eb6d2cc2ddcd6028917aeff8d76cd55185acb995ff3074c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119668 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n![](images/e1/e1b3730913029ed000c4ef86338bcd8be0fff045cda12131f894a1818996521b.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n![](images/a7/a7d5c905d9f78444b00c51a1b641d132365f5fc8619ad00fc0fd5fc27942709c.svg)\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n![](images/f1/f1d0656f8aabffd2d3e2bee903f3763aa610a4731991669a625e473262d7ea32.svg)\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n![](images/00/00c52c08ef614e87fe29584d994cec16298fa1f3bf02a1b4c6b7463bede7a6d1.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119792 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186377107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395818 #edb/re