\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468771 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468772 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468773 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n- **I.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345029 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468774 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468775 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468776 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98334c69-2d0e-5